#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133513 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f260476d-35d0-504a-9e0a-86f7fb0049bd" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Projection of a Vector onto a One-Dimensional Subspace"}] :question/id #uuid "1ad2672a-5773-5f2e-ab81-64e51ab4a7e2" :question/math-academy-id "e-5570" :question/problem "Let $\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 9 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ Find the orthogonal projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{\\mathbf{b}\\}$." :question/worked-solution "The orthogonal projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{b}\\}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nproj_{b}a &= \\frac{b \\cdot a}{b \\cdot b}b \\\\\n&= (2 \\cdot 2 + 1 \\cdot 9 + 0 \\cdot (-1) + 1 \\cdot (-1))/(2^{2} + 1^{2} + 0^{2} + 1^{2})b \\\\\n&= \\frac{4 + 9 + 0 - 1}{4 + 1 + 0 + 1}b \\\\\n&= \\frac{12}{6}b \\\\\n&= 2b \\\\\n&= 2\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133514 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "144d1258-f8d5-55e9-be38-ec25b6f84bd3" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Distance  Between a Vector and a One-Dimensional Subspace"}] :question/id #uuid "2031cdd7-c43e-509c-8bb2-4d7362914e7b" :question/math-academy-id "e-5571" :question/problem "Let $\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$ If $\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{y} + \\mathbf{z}$, where $\\mathbf{y} \\in \\text{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}\\}$ and $\\mathbf{z} \\perp \\text{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}\\}$, then find $\\mathbf{z}$." :question/worked-solution "A diagram of the situation is provided below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2122/Source/Images/1a8d9c2a40956965eaef1293fc216d15.png)\n\nFirst, we find the orthogonal projection of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}\\}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nproj_{a}x &= \\frac{a \\cdot x}{a \\cdot a}a \\\\\n&= (1 \\cdot 4 + (-1) \\cdot (-4) + 0 \\cdot 2 + (-1) \\cdot 2)/(1^{2} + (-1)^{2} + 0^{2} + (-1)^{2})a \\\\\n&= \\frac{4 + 4 + 0 - 2}{1 + 1 + 0 + 1}a \\\\\n&= \\frac{6}{3}a \\\\\n&= 2a \\\\\n&= 2\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= proj_{a}x = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\nz &= x - proj_{a}x = \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} - \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\mathbf{y} \\in \\text{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}\\}$ and $\\mathbf{z} \\perp \\text{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}\\}$, as shown in the picture above."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133515 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f8d8c7fb-e69a-5b52-9566-4d2e0c7e54fc" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Projection of a Vector onto the Solution Space of a System of Equations"}] :question/id #uuid "5168f07e-4a22-5e0c-a612-e5c6f77f99eb" :question/math-academy-id "e-5572" :question/problem "Given the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ and the system of linear equations below, find the projection of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto the solution space of the system.\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-10 \\\\ 20 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the basis of the solution space of the system.\n\nConsider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system, which we reduce to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{2} \\mid:=R_{2} - R_{1} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are $2$ pivot columns ($1$st and $2$nd). Thus, $x_3$ is a free variable. Now, from the second equation, we get $x_2=4x_3$.\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\nx_1+4x_3+4x_3= 0 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad x_1=-8x_3\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-8x_{3} \\\\ 4x_{3} \\\\ x_{3}\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsequently, $\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ is a basis of the solution space of the system.\n\nFinally, we find the projection of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto the solution space of the system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nproj_{b}x &= \\frac{x \\cdot b}{b \\cdot b}b \\\\\n&= ((-10) \\cdot (-8) + 20 \\cdot 4 + 2 \\cdot 1)/((-8)^{2} + 4^{2} + 1^{2})b \\\\\n&= \\frac{80 + 80 + 2}{64 + 16 + 1}b \\\\\n&= \\frac{162}{81}b \\\\\n&= 2b \\\\\n&= 2\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-16 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134158 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "144d1258-f8d5-55e9-be38-ec25b6f84bd3" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Distance  Between a Vector and a One-Dimensional Subspace"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bd8ace83-da01-51dc-8b7c-5c6c39cf2c34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131391} {:answer/id #uuid "c89cbf41-ee8b-5e84-88fd-7b88b0ee146d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -9 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131392} {:answer/id #uuid "ea496413-55f6-513d-8635-c1ccb5f3cc52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131393} {:answer/id #uuid "2011453f-5f42-511b-824e-e7871b71ed24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131394} {:answer/id #uuid "57361eb1-cf6b-5408-9965-10